Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     TextData["Relations"]], "paclet:ChernSimons/ref/Relations", 
    "ChernSimons Package Symbol"},
   "PackageLink",
   BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "97f7fca1-d391-4bab-925f-5958385b9bb6"],
 " gives the following relations and arities:"
}], "Notes",
 CellFrameLabels->{{Inherited, Inherited}, {Inherited, 
    Cell[
     TextData[{
      "26.09.30: Design review - one function names the relations of every \
structure, so that a single loop, Obstruction[s, name, t] over Tuples[basis, \
arity] for each entry of Relations[s], runs on every structure (R5e, \
2026-09-30). The arity is the number of arguments Obstruction takes: All for \
the A-infinity relations, which have one relation of each arity, says that \
without claiming infinitely many arguments, and 0 for the own axioms of a \
decomposition, which are conditions on its parts in every degree rather than \
identities on arguments, keeps the loop running since Tuples[basis, 0] is \
{{}} (R5g, 2026-09-30). The relations of a complex are one per axiom the old \
recognizer CochainComplexWithPairingQ tested. It replaces the Association \
$DefiningIdentities, which carried each identity of the string algebra with \
its arity and its function. A Maurer-Cartan element is not a structure, and \
there is no Relations of an element. The function is declared with \
Obstruction and RelationsQ in the HomotopyAlgebras part, and the \
string-algebra and complex rules are put on it by the parts that define those \
structures. No alternative name was recorded. Prior art: the Wolfram Language \
has no symbol listing the defining relations of an algebraic structure.", 
       "\n\n", 
       Cell[
        BoxData[
         GridBox[{{
            Cell[
             BoxData[
              ButtonBox[
               DynamicBox[
                FEPrivate`FrontEndResource[
                "DocumentationToolsStrings", "Edit"]], BaseStyle -> 
